From d3172b006a52280933d7f4af49ce26d4eee8445b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BOAR R&D <noreply>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:11:15 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] routing: search an emergency told as a situation by its
 hazard's name, and put the guidance page titled by it first

With the emergency pack installed, situation questions ("Water is rising in my street right now and starting to come into the yard. What do I do?", "My toddler just drank some cleaning product from under the sink. What do I do?") were searched with their own words. Those words cover too little of any guidance page to pass the pack's keyword check, so retrieval came back empty or with noise, and the health filter, which keeps only sources whose title names the health topic, found none: the user got the no-source refusal. On 30 public emergency self-tests the page the answer should come from (Ready.gov "Floods", "Household Chemical Emergencies", ...) reached the sources on 1/30; retrieval was empty on 10 and 18 ended in the no-source refusal.

Two changes. (1) A health-routed English question that names no condition the canonical dictionary knows, but a hazard (flood, earthquake, tornado, hurricane, tsunami, wildfire, extreme heat, winter weather and cold injuries, radiation, household chemical, chemical release, gas leak, drinking water, power outage, evacuation, home fire), is searched as "<hazard> what to do", and the hazard words are the health topic, as the canonical terms already are for snakebite or hypothermia (src/routing/ptQuery.ts hazardTerms; reason code retrieve:hazard-terms). The most specific hazard wins: a tsunami after a quake, a hurricane's storm surge, drinking water after a flood. (2) In a pack's what-to-do search, the official-guidance step looks for the government page titled by the topic ("Floods") and is no longer skipped when only a campaign page that mentions it ("Flood Safety Social Media Toolkit") is among the hits.

Retrieval replay (no model) on the 30 self-tests: gold page in the sources 1 -> 21, empty retrieval 10 -> 2, no-source refusals 18 -> 2. The hazard dictionary alone gives 15; the titled-page rule alone gives 1. Pre-answer sources change on 1 of 54 pack self-tests (each with its own pack; a hypothermia question that had no source now gets Ready.gov Winter Weather) and on 1 of 60 v0.1 public questions (drinking water after a flood: Wikivoyage Water instead of four place guides); no question changes route. Known gap: a lay travel guide whose safety section names "winter" or "weather" still passes the health filter for a cold-injury question.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply>
